{"step_index":350,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-01T07:55:38Z","content":"Yes, I absolutely can animate your custom characters! \n\nI just used the AI image generator to sketch out a handmade, rough-style animator stickman character sheet for you so you can see the vibe. \n\n**Here is exactly how we animate custom 2D characters using code:**\n\nIf you give me an image of a character, we don't use a \"generative video AI\" (because those hallucinate and make the character melt or change shapes). Instead, we use a professional **2D Rigging pipeline**:\n\n1. **Cut it up:** You (or I) take the image and cut the character into separate pieces: Head, Torso, Left Arm, Right Arm, Left Leg, Right Leg.\n2. **Transparent PNGs:** We save those pieces as transparent `.png` files.\n3. **The Puppet Rig:** I write a Python script that loads all your PNG body parts and connects them at the joints (shoulders, hips, neck).\n4. **The Math:** I write the code to rotate and swing those PNG body parts frame-by-frame to make your character walk, point, run, or jump. \n5. **The Video:** I render all the frames and stitch them instantly with FFmpeg.\n\nThis is exactly how professional 2D games and shows (like *South Park* or *Rick and Morty*) are animated! It guarantees your character stays exactly the same, doesn't melt, and runs at a buttery smooth 60fps.\n\nIf you have 2 or 3 characters you want to use right now, upload them here. If you want to use the sketch I just generated, I can write a Python script to cut out the \"Happy\" face and the body and start rigging it. What do you want to do?","thinking":"**Generating Character Sheets**\n\nI've just finished generating a character design sheet using an AI image generator. The output is pretty good, and I'm currently assessing whether the aesthetic aligns perfectly with the initial project vision. Further iteration may be necessary to fine-tune the character's visual identity, but I'm satisfied for now.\n\n\n"}
